# skill-sync (Cursor)

Keeps skills/agents/commands installed in a project in sync with this
catalog's source, without silently overwriting a repo's own
customizations. This is the *update* half of the lifecycle —
`INSTALL.md` is the initial copy, `agent-doctor` verifies an installed
agent actually runs on its platform, this skill checks whether an
already-installed file is stale relative to where it came from.

## Why a lockfile, not a blind re-copy

Re-copying every file from the catalog would silently destroy any
project-scoped override. Telling "the user changed this on purpose" apart
from "this just hasn't been updated since install" requires knowing what
the file looked like **at install time** — hence the lockfile, not just
current-project-file vs current-catalog-file.

## Step 0 — Locate the lockfile(s) and confirm scope

Installs can be project-level or global — check both, don't assume one:

- Project: `.agent-skills-lock.json` at the project root.
- Global: `~/.agent-skills-lock.json` (one shared file regardless of which
  platform(s) were chosen at install time).

Only one exists → use it. Both exist → ask the user which scope they mean
(project, global, or both) before going further; don't silently pick one.
Neither exists → don't guess a baseline. Tell the user no tracked installs
were found and offer: (a) rebuild a lockfile now (ask which scope) from
current file content as the new baseline, or (b) run ad-hoc mode — diff
each named file's current content against the catalog's current content,
asking before every overwrite.

Once resolved, read the lockfile(s) — each entry has the skill/agent name,
installed file paths, `source_url`, `source_commit` at install time, and a
content hash per file as it stood right after copying.

## Step 1 — Ask which tracked skills/agents to sync

Unless a specific name was already given, list every entry from the
lockfile(s) resolved in Step 0 as an interactive multi-select checklist
(group by scope if both project and global apply) and let the user pick
which ones to actually sync — don't process every tracked entry unasked.
If a specific name was given, sync just that one (validate it's actually
tracked first).

## Step 2 — Resolve the source

- **GitHub source (preferred, no clone needed):** use the GitHub API to
  read each tracked file's blob SHA / latest commit SHA directly, compare
  against the lockfile baseline, and only fetch raw content for files
  whose SHA actually changed.
- **Non-GitHub source, or API unreachable:** fall back to a local clone —
  `git pull` an existing cached clone, or clone fresh and record the path
  in the lockfile.
- Never diff with no commit/SHA reference at all — Step 3 needs a real
  "changed since baseline" signal.

## Step 3 — Classify each selected file

Compare baseline hash vs current project file vs current source file, for
files belonging to a skill/agent selected in Step 1:

| Project vs baseline | Source vs baseline | Meaning | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| unchanged | unchanged | nothing moved | skip |
| unchanged | changed | upstream updated, project never touched | auto-update |
| changed | unchanged | project-scoped override | skip, report as intentional |
| changed | changed | both diverged | conflict — show diff, ask user, never auto-resolve |
| any | removed/renamed upstream | deleted/split/renamed in catalog | never silently delete — ask |

Also track any **OpenCode command wrapper** the lockfile lists for a
skill (`.opencode/commands/<name>.md`) — its frontmatter `description` is
copied from `CATALOG.md` at install time, so it can go stale even when
the skill's own file didn't move. Diff it against a freshly-generated
wrapper for the current `CATALOG.md` description and classify with the
same rules. Don't invent a wrapper that was never installed.

## Step 4 — Apply and re-baseline

For every file actually updated: write the new content, then update its
lockfile hash and `source_commit`. Skipped files (unchanged, or kept
override) keep their entry untouched. Delete a removed-upstream file only
after the user confirms.

## Step 5 — Report

One summary: which scope(s) were synced, up to date, auto-updated (list),
skipped as intentional overrides (list), removed/renamed upstream (list,
with the user's choice), and conflicts still pending (list, with what was
asked).

## Not this skill's job

- Not the initial install — that's `INSTALL.md`'s job.
- Not a way to force-overwrite a project's customizations.
- Not `agent-doctor` — that verifies an installed agent runs on its
  platform; this only checks staleness vs source.
